+//! `onsa fmt` takes off the parentheses that are redundant, and no others (W3-12, unit U2;
+//! `docs/onsa-tools.md` §3.2, §3.3, §3.4 and §3.5, spec §0.2 (P2), §2.4, §2.5, §3.1, §4, §6.1, §7, §11.3 and
+//! §18.2; plan S-115, S-45, S-336, S-339, S-340 and S-497).
+//!
+//! The rule (`docs/onsa-tools.md` §3.3, S-339): a pair of parentheses is taken off when the tree is the same
+//! without it and the syntax stage finds no error in the result. The strength of the expression in the pair
+//! (postfix > prefix > `as` and `at` > the binary operators > the range symbols, spec §3.1) is compared with the
+//! strength that the place asks for. The binary operators are in groups with a partial order
+//! (multiplicative > additive > comparison > `&&` and `||`, remainder > comparison); a pair that would make a
+//! clash of groups (E0010) stays. A pair inside the same group stays as the writer's grouping (the only
+//! exception to the rule). The pairs that the tree needs (`(a + b) * c`, `(-x).max(1)`), and the pairs whose
+//! removal is an error of the syntax stage (E0010, E0011 and E0012, a struct literal or a block in the head of
+//! `if`, `while`, `match`, `for` or `par`), stay too. The whole of a statement, an argument, an element, a head
+//! and an end of a range (S-336) loses its pair; so do the pairs round a name, a literal, a postfix
+//! expression and a pair, and a type or a pattern that is only grouped (spec §2.4: `(e)` is grouping in an
+//! expression, a pattern and a type).
+//!
+//! What is here, in the order of the places (every test has the whole text of the input and of the expected
+//! output, worked out from the rules above and not taken from the output of `fmt`):
+//! - the pairs that go: a stronger group in a weaker one, every pair of the order (A); a prefix expression as
+//!   the operand of a binary operator, `as` and `at` (B); a name, a literal, a postfix expression and a pair
+//!   in a pair, and the nesting of pairs, which is gone in one pass (C); the whole of a statement, an
+//!   argument, an element, a field, an index, the tail of a block, an arm and a guard, an `assert` (D); the
+//!   heads of `if`, `while`, `match` and `for`, and the pair round a call that holds a struct literal (E);
+//!   the ends of a range (F); a type (G); a pattern (H); the pairs that go and stay in one expression (I);
+//!   the line breaks and the comments inside a pair (J);
+//! - the pairs that stay (K): the same group, the tree, the clash of groups, `as` and `at`, a stack of prefix
+//!   operators, the head with a struct literal or a block, a function type before `uses` and `at`, a comment
+//!   before `as`. They pass now (nothing is taken off) and keep a `fmt` that takes off too much honest; the
+//!   cases of the pairs that stay and are accepted by the syntax stage are also in
+//!   `tests/spec/fmt/parens_*.onsa`;
+//! - the files `fmt` does not write: `(s.f)(x)` and `for i in (0 ..< n)` (L, spec §6.1 and §7: errors of the
+//!   syntax stage; `fmt` ends with 2, writes nothing, takes nothing off elsewhere in the file either);
+//! - `onsa diff --ast` (M): two files that differ only in a redundant pair are not different (`(a * b) + c`
+//!   and `a * b + c`), and two files that differ in a pair that the tree needs are different.
+//!
+//! Every case is run by `onsa fmt` on a file in a temporary directory. The test checks the exit code 0, the
+//! whole text of the output, that the output is a fixed point (`fmt --check` ends with 0 and another `fmt`
+//! changes nothing), that `onsa diff --ast <copy of the input> <output>` finds no difference (exit code 0;
+//! `fmt` and `diff --ast` use the same comparison, spec `docs/onsa-tools.md` §3.5), and that the output
+//! passes `onsa check` as the input does. Before `fmt` runs, the input and the expected text are checked to
+//! have no error of the syntax stage (a file with one is not rewritten, `onsa fmt` would end with 2, §18.2)
+//! and to pass `onsa check`; a case that uses a form which `onsa check` of this version refuses in a later
+//! stage (E0200 of the clock of a computed expression, the closed range in a head) is marked as `syntax only`
+//! and is checked for the syntax stage only.
+//!
+//! What is not here, and why:
+//! - the rewrites of U1 (the `return` at the end, `-> ()`, `uses {}`, `init`, `move`): two cases combine a
+//!   `return (a + b)` at the end with the pair (U1 and U2 together);
+//! - the text of the diagnostics of the groups (`operator_groups.rs`) and of the ranges (`range_syntax.rs`);
+//! - whether `onsa diff --ast` reports a difference in an ordinary comment (S-534);
+//! - what is not decided by the spec (the report of the writer of this file lists them): the pair round a
+//!   same-group operand for `diff --ast`, a line break right after `(` or right before `)` of a pair that
+//!   goes, a line of a comment alone inside a pair that goes, and the strength of `if`, `match`, a closure
+//!   and the other forms that are not an operand of the order of §3.1 as the operand of an operator.
+//!
+//! A test that needs the removal of the pairs runs ignored until W3-12 (unit U2) lands; each ignore starts
+//! with the work.
+//!
+//! Every test uses only the binary.
+
+use std::path::PathBuf;
+use std::process::{Command, Output};
+use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
+
+const ONSA: &str = env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_onsa");
+
+struct Dir(PathBuf);
+
+impl Dir {
+    fn new(tag: &str) -> Dir {
+        // The counter makes the directory the test's own even when two tests use the same tag.
+        static NEXT: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
+        let n = NEXT.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
+        let d = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!("onsa_fmt_parens_{}_{n}_{tag}", std::process::id()));
+        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&d);
+        std::fs::create_dir_all(&d).unwrap();
+        Dir(d)
+    }
+
+    fn file(&self, name: &str, text: &str) -> String {
+        let p = self.0.join(name);
+        std::fs::write(&p, text).unwrap();
+        p.to_string_lossy().into_owned()
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for Dir {
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&self.0);
+    }
+}
+
+fn onsa(args: &[&str]) -> Output {
+    Command::new(ONSA).args(args).output().expect("run onsa")
+}
+
+fn code(out: &Output) -> i32 {
+    out.status.code().unwrap_or_else(|| panic!("ended by a signal: {out:?}"))
+}
+
+fn read(path: &str) -> String {
+    std::fs::read_to_string(path).unwrap()
+}
+
+/// Everything the command wrote, the standard output and the standard error.
+fn all_text(out: &Output) -> String {
+    format!("{}{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout), String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr))
+}
+
+/// The lines joined, with one line break at the end of the file.
+fn src(lines: &[&str]) -> String {
+    let mut s = lines.join("\n");
+    s.push('\n');
+    s
+}
+
+/// Several runs of lines as one text.
+fn src_cat(parts: &[&[&str]]) -> String {
+    let all: Vec<&str> = parts.iter().flat_map(|p| p.iter().copied()).collect();
+    src(&all)
+}
+
+/// A file before and after `onsa fmt`: the whole text of each.
+struct Case {
+    what: &'static str,
+    input: String,
+    expected: String,
+    /// `onsa check` of this version refuses the program in a stage after the syntax stage (a form that is
+    /// not lowered yet); the syntax stage finds nothing.
+    syntax_only: bool,
+}
+
+fn case(what: &'static str, input: &[&str], expected: &[&str]) -> Case {
+    Case { what, input: src(input), expected: src(expected), syntax_only: false }
+}
+
+/// A case whose input is already the output: nothing is to be taken off.
+fn kept(what: &'static str, lines: &[&str]) -> Case {
+    Case { what, input: src(lines), expected: src(lines), syntax_only: false }
+}
+
+impl Case {
+    fn syntax_only(mut self) -> Case {
+        self.syntax_only = true;
+        self
+    }
+}
+
+/// Items that the bodies of the cases use (canonical: `fmt` leaves them as they are).
+const PRELUDE: &[&str] = &[
+    "pub struct Pt {",
+    "  x: I32,",
+    "  y: I32,",
+    "}",
+    "",
+    "fn id(a: I32) -> I32 {",
+    "  a",
+    "}",
+    "",
+    "fn two(a: I32, b: I32) -> I32 {",
+    "  a + b",
+    "}",
+    "",
+    "fn make(a: I32) -> Pt {",
+    "  Pt { x: a, y: a }",
+    "}",
+    "",
+    "fn px(p: Pt) -> I32 {",
+    "  p.x",
+    "}",
+    "",
+];
+
+/// A case with the items of `PRELUDE` in front of the body.
+fn prel(what: &'static str, input: &[&str], expected: &[&str]) -> Case {
+    Case { what, input: src_cat(&[PRELUDE, input]), expected: src_cat(&[PRELUDE, expected]), syntax_only: false }
+}
+
+fn prel_kept(what: &'static str, lines: &[&str]) -> Case {
+    prel(what, lines, lines)
+}
+
+/// The text passes the syntax stage (`fmt --check` does not end with 2 or 101) and, unless the case is
+/// marked, `onsa check` (exit code 0).
+fn assert_valid(d: &Dir, name: &str, c: &Case, which: &str, text: &str) {
+    let p = d.file(name, text);
+    let out = onsa(&["fmt", "--check", &p]);
+    let k = code(&out);
+    assert!(k == 0 || k == 1, "{}: the {which} text has a diagnostic of the syntax stage: {out:?}\n{text}", c.what);
+    if which == "expected" {
+        // The expected text is the canonical form: the layout of it is one that `fmt` itself leaves alone.
+        assert_eq!(k, 0, "{}: the expected text is not canonical for `fmt --check`: {out:?}\n{text}", c.what);
+    }
+    if !c.syntax_only {
+        let out = onsa(&["check", &p]);
+        assert_eq!(code(&out), 0, "{}: `onsa check` finds a problem in the {which} text: {out:?}\n{text}", c.what);
+    }
+}
+
+/// Runs `onsa fmt` on every input (one command, so the files are written one after the other), expects
+/// the exit code 0, compares the whole text of each output, and checks that the output is a fixed point,
+/// that `onsa check` is still happy with it, and that `onsa diff --ast` finds no difference between the
+/// input and the output.
+fn expect_fmt(tag: &str, cases: &[Case]) {
+    assert!(!cases.is_empty(), "no case to run");
+    let d = Dir::new(tag);
+    for (i, c) in cases.iter().enumerate() {
+        assert_valid(&d, &format!("e{i}.onsa"), c, "expected", &c.expected);
+        assert_valid(&d, &format!("i{i}.onsa"), c, "input", &c.input);
+    }
+    let originals: Vec<String> =
+        cases.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, c)| d.file(&format!("o{i}.onsa"), &c.input)).collect();
+    let paths: Vec<String> = cases.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, c)| d.file(&format!("c{i}.onsa"), &c.input)).collect();
+    let mut args = vec!["fmt"];
+    args.extend(paths.iter().map(String::as_str));
+    let out = onsa(&args);
+    assert_eq!(code(&out), 0, "onsa fmt must write the files (101 is the check before writing): {out:?}");
+    let mut wrong = Vec::new();
+    for (c, path) in cases.iter().zip(&paths) {
+        let got = read(path);
+        if got != c.expected {
+            wrong.push(format!("{}\n--- input\n{}--- expected\n{}--- got\n{got}", c.what, c.input, c.expected));
+        }
+    }
+    assert!(wrong.is_empty(), "{} of {} files are not as expected:\n{}", wrong.len(), cases.len(), wrong.join("\n"));
+    for ((c, path), orig) in cases.iter().zip(&paths).zip(&originals) {
+        let written = read(path);
+        let out = onsa(&["fmt", "--check", path]);
+        assert_eq!(code(&out), 0, "{}: the output is not a fixed point of fmt --check: {out:?}", c.what);
+        let out = onsa(&["fmt", path]);
+        assert_eq!(code(&out), 0, "{}: {out:?}", c.what);
+        assert_eq!(read(path), written, "{}: a second fmt changes the output", c.what);
+        if !c.syntax_only {
+            let out = onsa(&["check", path]);
+            assert_eq!(code(&out), 0, "{}: the output does not pass `onsa check`: {out:?}", c.what);
+        }
+        let out = onsa(&["diff", "--ast", orig, path]);
+        assert_eq!(
+            code(&out),
+            0,
+            "{}: `diff --ast` reports the input and the output as different: {}",
+            c.what,
+            all_text(&out)
+        );
+    }
+}
+
+/// The exit code and the whole output of `onsa diff --ast` on two texts.
+fn diff_of(d: &Dir, tag: &str, a: &str, b: &str) -> (i32, String) {
+    let pa = d.file(&format!("{tag}_a.onsa"), a);
+    let pb = d.file(&format!("{tag}_b.onsa"), b);
+    for p in [&pa, &pb] {
+        let out = onsa(&["check", p]);
+        assert_eq!(code(&out), 0, "the text of the pair must pass `onsa check`: {out:?}\n{}", read(p));
+    }
+    let out = onsa(&["diff", "--ast", &pa, &pb]);
+    (code(&out), all_text(&out))
+}
+
+// ===================================================================================================
+// A. A stronger group in a weaker one (docs/onsa-tools.md §3.3, spec §3.1: S-45, S-115)
+// ===================================================================================================
+
+/// The strengths of §3.1 that can be written with types that check: multiplicative > additive
+/// (`(a * b) + c`), additive > comparison (`(a + 1) < n`), remainder > comparison (`(a % 2) == 0`),
+/// multiplicative > comparison (through the chain of the order), comparison > `&&` and `||`. Each is on
+/// the left and on the right. (Additive, multiplicative and remainder in `&&` and `||` are no `Bool`.)
+fn c_strong_groups() -> Vec<Case> {
+    vec![
+        case(
+            "multiplicative in additive, on both sides",
+            &[
+                "pub fn arithmetic(a: I32, b: I32, c: I32) -> I32 {",
+                "  let p = (a * b) + c",
+                "  let q = c + (a * b)",
+                "  let r = (a / b) - c",
+                "  let s = c - (a / b)",
+                "  let t = (a * b) + (c / a) + (b * c)",
+                "  p + q + r + s + t",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn arithmetic(a: I32, b: I32, c: I32) -> I32 {",
+                "  let p = a * b + c",
+                "  let q = c + a * b",
+                "  let r = a / b - c",
+                "  let s = c - a / b",
+                "  let t = a * b + c / a + b * c",
+                "  p + q + r + s + t",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        case(
+            "wrapping and saturating forms follow their group",
+            &[
+                "pub fn wrapping(a: U8, b: U8, c: U8) -> U8 {",
+                "  let p = (a *% b) +% c",
+                "  let q = c -| (a *| b)",
+                "  let r = (a / b) +| c",
+                "  p +% q +% r",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn wrapping(a: U8, b: U8, c: U8) -> U8 {",
+                "  let p = a *% b +% c",
+                "  let q = c -| a *| b",
+                "  let r = a / b +| c",
+                "  p +% q +% r",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        case(
+            "additive, remainder and multiplicative in comparison, on both sides",
+            &[
+                "pub fn comparisons(a: I32, b: I32, c: I32, n: I32) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = (a + 1) < n",
+                "  let q = n >= (a + b)",
+                "  let r = (a % 2) == 0",
+                "  let s = c != (a % 2)",
+                "  let t = (a * b) <= c",
+                "  let u = c > (a * b)",
+                "  let v = (a - b) == (c + n)",
+                "  p && q && r && s && t && u && v",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn comparisons(a: I32, b: I32, c: I32, n: I32) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = a + 1 < n",
+                "  let q = n >= a + b",
+                "  let r = a % 2 == 0",
+                "  let s = c != a % 2",
+                "  let t = a * b <= c",
+                "  let u = c > a * b",
+                "  let v = a - b == c + n",
+                "  p && q && r && s && t && u && v",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        case(
+            "multiplicative, remainder and additive in && and || (through the chain of the order; no Bool, so the syntax stage only)",
+            &[
+                "pub fn through_the_chain(a: I32, b: I32, c: Bool) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = (a * b) && c",
+                "  let q = c || (a * b)",
+                "  let r = (a + b) && c",
+                "  let s = c || (a + b)",
+                "  let t = (a % b) && c",
+                "  let u = c || (a % b)",
+                "  p && q && r && s && t && u",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn through_the_chain(a: I32, b: I32, c: Bool) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = a * b && c",
+                "  let q = c || a * b",
+                "  let r = a + b && c",
+                "  let s = c || a + b",
+                "  let t = a % b && c",
+                "  let u = c || a % b",
+                "  p && q && r && s && t && u",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        )
+        .syntax_only(),
+        case(
+            "comparison in && and ||, on both sides",
+            &[
+                "pub fn logic(a: I32, b: I32, c: I32, d: I32) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = (a < b) && (c < d)",
+                "  let q = (a == b) || (c != d)",
+                "  let r = (a <= b) && c > d",
+                "  let s = a >= b || (c == d)",
+                "  let t = (a + b < c) && (d * 2 == a)",
+                "  let u = (a % 2 == 0) || (b % 2 == 0)",
+                "  p && q && r && s && t && u",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn logic(a: I32, b: I32, c: I32, d: I32) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = a < b && c < d",
+                "  let q = a == b || c != d",
+                "  let r = a <= b && c > d",
+                "  let s = a >= b || c == d",
+                "  let t = a + b < c && d * 2 == a",
+                "  let u = a % 2 == 0 || b % 2 == 0",
+                "  p && q && r && s && t && u",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+    ]
+}
+
+#[test]
+#[ignore = "W3-12 U2: fmt takes no parenthesis off yet, a stronger group in a weaker group (S-115, S-45, S-339)"]
+fn a_stronger_group_in_a_weaker_group_loses_its_parentheses() {
+    expect_fmt("strong_groups", &c_strong_groups());
+}
+
+// ===================================================================================================
+// B. A prefix expression and the operands of `as` and `at`
+// ===================================================================================================
+
+fn c_prefix_operands() -> Vec<Case> {
+    vec![
+        case(
+            "a prefix minus as the operand of a binary operator, on both sides",
+            &[
+                "pub fn prefix_operands(x: I32, y: I32) -> I32 {",
+                "  let p = (-x) + y",
+                "  let q = y - (-x)",
+                "  let r = (-x) * (-y)",
+                "  let s = y % (-x)",
+                "  p + q + r + s",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn prefix_operands(x: I32, y: I32) -> I32 {",
+                "  let p = -x + y",
+                "  let q = y - -x",
+                "  let r = -x * -y",
+                "  let s = y % -x",
+                "  p + q + r + s",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        case(
+            "a prefix not as the operand of && || and ==",
+            &[
+                "pub fn prefix_logic(c: Bool, d: Bool) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = (!c) && d",
+                "  let q = c || (!d)",
+                "  let r = (!c) == d",
+                "  p && q && r",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn prefix_logic(c: Bool, d: Bool) -> Bool {",
+                "  let p = !c && d",
+                "  let q = c || !d",
+                "  let r = !c == d",
+                "  p && q && r",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        prel(
+            "a name or a postfix expression as the operand of a prefix",
+            &[
+                "pub fn prefix_operand(x: I32, p: Pt, xs: [I32; 3], i: U32, flag: Bool) -> I32 {",
+                "  let a = -(x)",
+                "  let b = -(xs[i])",
+                "  let c = -(p.x)",
+                "  let d = -(id(x))",
+                "  let e = !(flag)",
+                "  if e {",
+                "    a + b + c + d",
+                "  } else {",
+                "    0",
+                "  }",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn prefix_operand(x: I32, p: Pt, xs: [I32; 3], i: U32, flag: Bool) -> I32 {",
+                "  let a = -x",
+                "  let b = -xs[i]",
+                "  let c = -p.x",
+                "  let d = -id(x)",
+                "  let e = !flag",
+                "  if e {",
+                "    a + b + c + d",
+                "  } else {",
+                "    0",
+                "  }",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        prel(
+            "a prefix, a name and a postfix expression as the operand of as",
+            &[
+                "pub fn as_operands(x: I32, t: (I32, I32)) -> F64 {",
+                "  let a = (-x) as F64",
+                "  let b = (x) as F64",
+                "  let c = (t.0) as F64",
+                "  let d = (id(x)) as F64",
+                "  a + b + c + d",
+                "}",
+            ],
+            &[
+                "pub fn as_operands(x: I32, t: (I32, I32)) -> F64 {",
+                "  let a = -x as F64",
+                "  let b = x as F64",
+                "  let c = t.0 as F64",
+                "  let d = id(x) as F64",
+                "  a + b + c + d",
+                "}",
+            ],
+        ),
+        case(
+            "a name as the operand of at",
+            &["pub flow promote(p: F32 at block) -> F32 at sample {", "  let q = (p) at sample", "  q", "}"],
+            &["pub flow promote(p: F32 at block) -> F32 at sample {", "  let q = p at sample", "  q", "}"],
+        ),
+        case(
+            "a prefix as the operand of at",
+            &["pub flow promote(p: F32 at block) -> F32 at sample {", "  let q = (-p) at sample", "  q", "}"],
+            &["pub flow promote(p: F32 at block) -> F32 at sample {", "  let q = -p at sample", "  q", "}"],
+        )
+        .syntax_only(),
+    ]
